[P1] ZH16-01- Urban rooftop rainfall collection experiment A vocational college conducted a small-scale rainfall collection experiment on the roof of a school building to get students to observe the relationship between rainfall, roof area and water storage. The experimental system does not connect to the residential water supply network and provides non-potable water only for green irrigation and equipment cleaning. The roof has an effective drainage area of about 86 square meters, and a removable flush net has been installed at the front of the falling water pipes, and an initial drainage system has been installed. On June 18, 2026, when the first full rainfall was recorded, the students on duty simultaneously transcribed the rainfall and the water level of the water tank stored at the school's weather station. Filtering and recording the first two rains exposed a simple problem: the leaves on the netting significantly slowed the rate of water entry. In a single 12.6 mm rainfall, theoretically the water collected does not enter the reservoir completely, and the overflow drains away from near the roof. After cleaning the netting, the maintenance crew adds a thicker layer of barrier netting above the inlet to stop the large debris before it reaches the net. The modified system operates more steadily in the next 8.4 mm of rainfall, and students are more aware of the two scenarios of insufficient rainfall in the area and blocked entrance of mud. The outer side of the tank is marked with a capacity mark, and each record includes the initial liquid level, the final liquid level, the amount of rainfall and the apparent overflow conditions. In order to avoid mistaking evaporation or previous residual water for the result of this rainfall, the course requires students to read the data in the same time window. The teacher also emphasized that the rainfall measurement is not exactly the same as the actual rainfall on the roof, and that wind direction, roof slope and initial runoff will all make a difference, so the focus of the experiment is to establish a repeatable recording method, rather than pursuing a fixed collection efficiency. A month later, the class adjusted the green water arrangement based on the data. Stored rainwater is preferred for use in flower beds near the school building, avoiding transportation by small trucks. A distinctive non-drinking water fountain logo was added to the system, and it was stipulated that the smell, color and condition of the container should be checked before re-use after a long period of no rain. This small-scale experiment shows students that even a simple water-saving facility needs filtering, labeling, maintenance, and data recording before it can be transformed from a demonstration device into a manageable engineering system.
