[P1] ZH16-02- A small unmanned riverboat mapping exercise for a university mapping society using a small unmanned boat for training in a closed managed artificial river. The vessels are equipped with satellite positioning modules, depth sensors and low-speed wireless links, and are tasked with recording depth changes along pre-planned routes, rather than conducting formal hydrological surveys. The training area is about 420 meters long, the water is calm, and there are shore observers at both ends. Before the dive, the operator checks the propeller, antenna and battery fastener and confirms that the manual take-off switch can stop automatically immediately. The first route consists of sixteen runways with a speed of 1.2 m/s. The drones are able to steadily track the path on open water, but there is a continuous deflection when approaching a concrete tunnel. The observer does not directly modify the depth measurement data, but first checks the position and body posture records. The results showed that the high walls near the tunnel reduced the quality of the satellite signal, while the side winds pushed the boat's bow outwards. The second voyage slowed down the area and moved the two points of navigation about 3 meters to the center of the canal, significantly reducing the error of course. Data processing at abnormalities also requires a quality check. The sensor occasionally gives sudden shallow readings in densely watered areas, which are inconsistent with the change in the anterior and posterior dots. The members of the society kept these original points on the map and did not directly delete them, with the markings indicating that the moss is suspected of reflecting water grass moss. They then sampled three locations from the shore using a ribbon gauge, confirming that two of the anomalies did not represent real riverbed swings. This process helps students understand that the data collected automatically still needs to be interpreted in conjunction with environmental and independent checks. At the end of the training, all flight tracks, depth records and operations logs are archived by date. The report specifically distinguishes between lithium sensor readings that have been verified in the field and abnormal values that have not yet been confirmed. The society plans to add a more rigorous repeat route test next time to compare differences in the same route at different wind speeds. This exercise does not seek beautiful 3D graphics, but instead focuses on secure takeover, exception retention and traceable data processing.
