[P1] ZH16-03- Winter maintenance on-site inspection process of a mountain automatic weather station An automatic weather station in the mountains performs annual maintenance before winter. The site is located at an altitude of about 2140 meters above sea level and records mainly temperature, relative humidity, wind speed, wind direction and precipitation. Due to the possibility of freezing roads in winter, maintenance workers hope to complete sensor inspection and backup power testing by the end of October. Upon arrival at the site, the staff takes pictures of the overall condition of the equipment and then checks whether the poles, the tow lines and the mine-proof connections are loose. All the torn screws are placed in a ribbon-covered box to avoid falling into debris and snow areas. Wind speed sensors are the focus of this test. In the last month, there have been two periods of sustained winds in the record. A small resistance can be felt when the windshield is turned on the spot, and when the protective cover is removed, it is found that there is a small accumulation of sand dust inside. After cleaning and reinstalling, the windshield rotates back to normal. Staff did not delete the original data of anomalies, but instead indicated the time of inspection and possible reasons in the maintenance logs, allowing subsequent analysts to determine which times should be used with caution. The power system consists of a solar panel, a charging controller and a battery. During the midday test, the panel surface was partially covered in thin snow, clearing the difference in charging current before and after. The battery has a standby voltage of 12.5 V, which remains stable after the access station load. Maintenance workers also inspected the waterproof connections and cable bends, as the low temperatures make the aging protective covers more likely to crack. Two straps showing signs of hardening are replaced and the stress release is re-fixed at the cable entrance. Before leaving the station, the team had the weather station run continuously for forty minutes, and the real-time data was easily compared with handheld thermometers and portable wind speedometers. This comparison does not replace formal calibration, but it does find a clear reduction in known risk and provides a clear background record for any subsequent abnormalities.
