[P1] ZH12-01- Campus lighting energy-efficient transformation of district testing
[P2] A comprehensive school is preparing to replace the corridor lighting in the two-storey school building, but the logistics department has chosen one floor to test the partitioning before the full procurement. There was a long use of lamps, uneven lighting in some areas, and at night there was often a situation where the whole floor was empty but the lights were on. The test area was divided into three categories of space: a common corridor, a stairway and a self-study area, each of which recorded lighting and electricity usage during the day, evening and night. The project requires that, while reducing energy consumption, no conspicuous dark areas can be created in the pursuit of energy savings. Normal corridors are equipped with efficient LED lighting and group controls, while the self-contained area maintains a high illumination. The staircase has an added presence sensor, but a delayed shutdown is set to prevent the lights from flashing repeatedly when personnel are stopped for a short time. In the early stages of the decoy, the sensor appears to have a blind spot at a corner, and students sometimes fail to trigger it in time when entering through a side door. Instead of simply improving the sensitivity of all sensors, the technicians changed the mounting angle of one of the probes and limited the detection range to the main passage area, reducing the mis-triggering of activity in adjacent rooms. Two-week run results
[P3] For two consecutive weeks, records show that the nighttime lighting consumption on the test floors decreased by about 31% compared to the same period before the renovation, but the logistics department did not directly extend this proportion to the entire building. Exam week and regular school week are different, and the weather also affects the time of day when lights need to be turned on. In order to obtain more comparable data, the next stage is planned to keep an unmodified area as a reference layer and record it in sync during the same class cycle. The self-study area also added a two-dimensional questionnaire code to collect subjective opinions on brightness and fluorescence. The illumination is checked using the same verified illuminator and the height and position of the measuring point are consistent. When a location is found below the design target, the staff first checks whether the lighting is covered by the sign and then decides whether to adjust the layout. The project also records the lamp model, rated power, installation date and controller number for easy tracking during subsequent maintenance. Such records are not considered to be of interest to aluminum once the energy-saving conversion of aluminum is completed, as control strategies and actual usage patterns may still change as the semester progresses. At the end of the trial, the logistics department divided the results into three categories: installation practices that could be directly replicated, control parameters that needed further observation, and local solutions that were not suitable for other areas. The final number of purchases will be decided based on the next round of testing. This process, while slower than a one-time replacement, reduces the risk of repeating the same error throughout the building and provides a clear baseline for later evaluation of energy savings.
