[P1] ZH12-02- A community library has installed a self-return device at the entrance of a community library to reduce queues at service desks during peak hours before and after closing. The device identifies the book by means of a radiofrequency tag, and after the reader places the book in the input slot, the screen displays the identification results and prints a short credential. Instead of cancelling the manual return window immediately, the library arranged a four-week trial run to record the success rate of identification, false alarms, bookcase capacity and reader operation time. The staff checks every day before opening the facility for unread cards or labels. The most common problem in the first week of adjusting the operating system is not a device failure, but the reader placing multiple books at once. While the system allows for continuous processing, when three or four books are stacked together, different labeling locations increase the likelihood of misses. The library then added a sign at the entrance, asked to put in one book at a time, and changed the screen prompt from a long sentence to a two-step short instruction. The second week saw a marked decrease in repetition. For older books that do not have RF labels, the device will explicitly prompt to turn over the artificial windows instead of treating them as unknown errors. The capacity of the bookcase also needs to be re-estimated. The original design was based on an average book thickness, but children's drawings and large picture books filled the space faster. After a weekend activity, the tank reached the alert line about 50 minutes before the expected time. Staff adjusted the frequency of the weekend clearance based on the records and added an 80% capacity reminder in the background. This reminder is for work only. Staff are still required to actually inspect the stacking inside the box, as the tilt of the book may affect mechanical measurements. To check for consistency in the accounting process, technicians randomly extract twenty self-refund records per day to check with the borrowing status in the bookkeeping system. When a timestamp is found to be inconsistent, the original logs are kept and network pacing is checked, rather than the reader record being directly modified. During the third week of the trial, there was a brief outage, the device switched to offline caching mode, and the network was restored and synced again. Staff then verified that all records in the cache queue had been successfully written. At the end of the four weeks, the self-help equipment was retained, but the artificial window remained open. The library believes that the criteria for success are not to completely replace the staff, but to make simple, standard book returns work more smoothly, while preserving a clear path for exceptional cases. The logs formed during the trial run also serve as a basis for subsequent maintenance, including information such as check-in times, synchronization interruptions, label read misses, and reader cancellations.
