[TITLE] ZH12-03- Regular patrol records of offshore fan blades
[P1] A small wind turbine at a coastal test site needs quarterly patrol. Long-term exposure to salt fog, strong winds and sand particles can lead to minor erosion of the surface of the leaf. The maintenance team chooses to check the shutdown in the morning at a low wind speed and disconnect the auto-start function according to the locking procedure. The ground crew first confirms that the leaflet has completely stopped, and then uses telescopes and high-resolution cameras to observe the front edges, roots and drainage holes of the three leaflets. The daytime ambient temperature is about 23°C, with an average wind speed of less than 4 m/s. Compared to the previous record, the second leaf has a light-colored stripe of abrasion 38 mm long near the tip of the leaf. The photos show that the damage is still limited to the surface coating, with no visible cracks. The team re-recorded the area with the same angle and scale and marked the location on the maintenance map. In order to avoid mistaking the light reflection for damage, the inspector took two more photographs from different directions. When all three sets of images see the same boundary, the point is added to the tracking list. The tight areas of the leaf roots show no signs of loosening, but one drainage hole is surrounded by salt crystals. The technician uses the specified soft tools to clean the surface without enlarging the porosity. The cabin shell and cable entrances are also checked at the same time, as water vapour in the seaward environment may enter from multiple locations. The humidity indicator in the control cabinet is still in the normal range, and there is no noticeable discoloration of the wiring terminals. Maintenance personnel recorded the items where no abnormal leaks were found, so that the next inspection would not be able to determine if something had been inspected. After the restoration of operation and the review of conditions, the fan was restored to low speed for fifteen minutes. Vibration and speed curves in the monitoring system did not differ significantly from before the shutdown. The team did not cancel the follow-up observation of the leaf tip wear as a result, but instead set the review time to six weeks later and stipulated that more detailed examinations should be scheduled in advance if the wear length increases by more than 10 mm or if cracks occur. This allows for clear trigger conditions to be maintained without excessive maintenance. The final record includes downtime, weather conditions, photo numbering, check items and pending items. All photos retain their original file and are not subject to incubation which would alter the details in the archived version. The value of a quarterly patrol is not only in discovering problems, but also in establishing continuous evidence that enables maintenance personnel to judge whether a surface change is stable, slow-moving or needs immediate treatment.
============================================================

